Now to understand why and how recursion works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:17.330 --> 01:41:23.930
we must first understand what a stack is. A stack&nbsp;
is a data structure that contains all of the tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:23.930 --> 01:41:29.120
you instruct your program to complete. Based on a&nbsp;
certain method, your program will then carry out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:29.120 --> 01:41:34.520
the tasks you give it is called a stack. Because&nbsp;
if we start another process before the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.520 --> 01:41:39.860
one completes, the process is stacked on top&nbsp;
of the other one, such as the animation on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.860 --> 01:41:46.190
screen is showing now, programs we write will&nbsp;
follow the LIFO structure. For those unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.190 --> 01:41:52.670
with accounting LIFO means lastin. First out, or&nbsp;
the last item put on the stack will be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:52.670 --> 01:41:58.730
one removed from it. Essentially, every time you&nbsp;
ask your computer to complete a task, that task is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:58.730 --> 01:42:03.710
added to the stack, and will be the first one to&nbsp;
be resolved. Think of it like a stack of stones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:03.710 --> 01:42:08.810
you can keep adding stones on top of your pile.&nbsp;
But in order to get to the one at the bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:08.810 --> 01:42:13.280
you must first remove all the rocks on top of&nbsp;
it. Now when your functions continually cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.280 --> 01:42:18.830
itself without end, without a base case, like in&nbsp;
our infinite loop example, then this stack will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:18.830 --> 01:42:24.410
never be resolved, as items will be continually&nbsp;
added to the stack without any of them ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:24.410 --> 01:42:30.140
completed. In this case, the memory allocated&nbsp;
to the stack exceeds the maximum allowed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:30.140 --> 01:42:37.100
a Stack Overflow error occurs resulting in your&nbsp;
program crashing. Think of this as if you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:37.100 --> 01:42:42.020
chores. And before you complete one chore, you&nbsp;
get called to do another chore. And then before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:42.020 --> 01:42:47.120
you can complete that one, you get called to do&nbsp;
another one. Since you keep stacking tasks, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:47.120 --> 01:42:52.910
shores on top of one another, the stack of tasks&nbsp;
will never be completed. And you will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:52.910 --> 01:42:58.430
die before ever finishing all of your chores.&nbsp;
This is the same logic that makes infinite loops&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:58.430 --> 01:43:04.400
crash your program. recursion works on these same&nbsp;
principles. The initial call makes a second call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:04.400 --> 01:43:09.680
which is added to the stack. And now that one&nbsp;
must be taken care of first. But in that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:09.680 --> 01:43:15.080
another function is called which gets added to&nbsp;
the stack, and so on until you reach the base&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:15.080 --> 01:43:21.560
case and what you slowly start going back down&nbsp;
the stack. In conclusion, recursion in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.560 --> 01:43:26.930
is extremely useful, because by calling the same&nbsp;
functions repeatedly, it breaks down the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.930 --> 01:43:32.690
